[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem: "Determine the maximum possible rank of a set such that the ranks [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof all its finite subsets are bounded by a given ordinal β."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me parse this. We have a set S, and we consider all finite subsets of S. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mEach finite subset has a "rank." We want the ranks of all finite subsets to be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbounded by β. We want to determine the maximum possible rank of S itself.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe rank of a set is defined inductively: rank(x) = sup{rank(y)+1 : y ∈ x}, and [0m[38;2;124;124;124mrank(∅) = 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a finite subset F = {x₁, ..., xₙ} of S, the rank of F is sup{rank(xᵢ)+1 : xᵢ[0m[38;2;124;124;124m∈ F} = max{rank(xᵢ)} + 1 (since F is finite, sup is max).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, actually rank(F) = sup{rank(xᵢ)+1 : xᵢ ∈ F}. If F is finite, this is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmax{rank(xᵢ)+1} = (max{rank(xᵢ)}) + 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo if all finite subsets F of S have rank(F) ≤ β, then for every finite subset [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{x₁,...,xₙ}, max{rank(xᵢ)+1} ≤ β, which means max{rank(xᵢ)} ≤ β - 1... wait, but[0m[38;2;124;124;124mβ is an ordinal, so we need to be careful.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mrank(F) = sup{rank(x)+1 : x ∈ F} ≤ β for all finite F ⊆ S.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis means for every x ∈ S, rank(x)+1 ≤ β (taking F = {x}), so rank(x) < β, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mi.e., rank(x) + 1 ≤ β.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, rank(x)+1 ≤ β means rank(x) < β.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider. If F = {x}, rank(F) = rank(x)+1. So rank(x)+1 ≤ β [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfor all x ∈ S, meaning rank(x) < β for all x ∈ S.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConversely, if rank(x) < β for all x ∈ S, then for any finite F, rank(F) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmax{rank(x)+1 : x ∈ F} ≤ β (since each rank(x)+1 ≤ β, and the max of finitely [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmany ordinals ≤ β is ≤ β).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but we need max{rank(x)+1} ≤ β. If each rank(x) < β, then rank(x)+1 ≤ β. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThe max of finitely many ordinals each ≤ β is ≤ β. So yes, rank(F) ≤ β.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the condition "ranks of all finite subsets bounded by β" is equivalent to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m"rank(x) < β for all x ∈ S."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, rank(S) = sup{rank(x)+1 : x ∈ S}.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe want to maximize rank(S) subject to rank(x) < β for all x ∈ S.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf rank(x) < β for all x ∈ S, then rank(x)+1 ≤ β for all x ∈ S, so sup{rank(x)+1[0m[38;2;124;124;124m: x ∈ S} ≤ β.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo rank(S) ≤ β.[0m

